{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.603,
            0.044,
            0.848,
            0.061
          ],
          "content": "Describing Motion Around Us"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "51 N"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.087,
            0.775,
            0.123
          ],
          "content": "only the numerical value (with units) is required, not the direction of motion. The **SI unit** for both is the **metre (m)**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.125,
            0.775,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "For example, in Fig. 4.4, between t = 0 s and t = 16 s, the total distance travelled by the athlete is 160 m, but her displacement is 40 m in the positive direction. We find that between these two instants, the total distance travelled and the magnitude of displacement are not equal. Can these quantities ever be equal?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.219,
            0.388,
            0.237
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 4.1: Let us analyse"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.24,
            0.775,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "1. As shown in Fig. 4.5, a ball is thrown vertically upwards from O. It moves up straight till B and then falls back to O. Can this be considered a motion in a straight line?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.295,
            0.56,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "2. For this motion, fill up the values in Table 4.1."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.338,
            0.771,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": "| S. No. | Position | Total distance travelled by the ball from O till that position | Displacement of the ball from O till that position |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. | O | 0 cm | 0 cm |\n| 2. | A | 40 cm | 40 cm in upward direction |\n| 3. | B |  |  |\n| 4. | C |  |  |\n| 5. | O |  |  |",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.193,
                0.319,
                0.689,
                0.335
              ],
              "content": "Table 4.1: Distance travelled and displacement of the ball"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_table_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.526,
            0.775,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "3. **Analyse** the data filled in Table 4.1 and choose which of the following is true for displacement:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.565,
            0.347,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "(i) It is never zero."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.581,
            0.739,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Its magnitude can be greater than the total distance travelled."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.598,
            0.762,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Its magnitude is less than or equal to the total distance travelled."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.615,
            0.763,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": "(iv) Its magnitude is less than the total distance travelled in all cases."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.803,
            0.085,
            0.858,
            0.1
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.779,
            0.325,
            0.955,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.782,
                0.571,
                0.965,
                0.654
              ],
              "content": "in vertical motion (two separate lines are shown only for clarity; in reality, the object goes up and falls back in the same straight line)"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.8,
            0.105,
            0.941,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": "For motion in a straight line, the total distance travelled and the magnitude of displacement are equal if the object moves without turning back, i.e., if it moves in one direction."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.649,
            0.188,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.82,
                0.556,
                0.927,
                0.57
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.5: A ball"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.192,
            0.658,
            0.382,
            0.677
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.687,
            0.876,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": "1. In the example of an athlete running back and forth on a straight track (Fig. 4.4), when will the displacement of the athlete be zero? What will be the total distance travelled in that case?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.737,
            0.847,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "2. Fuel used up in a vehicle depends on which of the following? Justify your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.756,
            0.399,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": "(i) Total distance travelled"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.775,
            0.319,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Displacement"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.794,
            0.506,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "3. A ball rolls down an inclined track as shown in Fig. 4.6. Is its motion, a straight line motion? Assuming the starting point of the ball (O) to be the origin, can its"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.505,
            0.761,
            0.868,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.527,
                0.833,
                0.85,
                0.849
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.6: A ball rolling down an inclined track"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_26.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.855,
            0.876,
            0.903
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "motion from O to D be depicted using a horizontal line as shown in Fig. 4.3? Are the values of total distance travelled and magnitude of displacement from O equal or different at positions A, B, C and D?"
        }
      ]
    }
